{"step_index":3557,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T10:03:56Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nThree.js is the right call. It gives you real lighting, bloom, and depth without writing raw WebGL shaders from scratch. You get the glow for free through post-processing passes, and your telemetry can drive material properties directly. The one thing to watch: Three.js orbit controls are built for camera navigation, not particle physics. You'll want a separate update loop for the orbs themselves so the camera and the particles don't fight each other. For your case, custom raw WebGL beats Three.js. Three.js is a convenience layer; it adds overhead, abstraction, and a learning curve your agents have to fight through. Raw WebGL it is. Your agents write the shaders, the old engine is live. \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-29T06:03:56-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
